zero-object-slots: a new object slot is cleared whole

Classification: harness plumbing.

FindEmptyObjectSlot() clears only the OBJECT_ATTRIBUTES of the slot it hands
out. TPM2_ContextSave() copies the whole slot (OBJECT or HASH_OBJECT) into the
context, so the parts the new occupant does not write still hold the octets of
an earlier occupant of the slot: e.g. a SHA-1 sequence object started in a
slot that held a SHA-384 sequence saves the tail of the old SHA512_CTX behind
its SHA_CTX. The saved context then depends on the slot's history, which
tpm-rs, which reproduces the reference's saved contexts byte for byte
(crates/tpm2-engine/src/handler/context_format.rs), does not track. The slot
is now cleared whole, as ibmswtpm2 (libtpms) and the version 185 reference
(whose FindEmptyObjectSlot() calls ObjectFlush(), which clears the slot)
already do. Nothing reads the stale octets, so no other value changes, and a
context saved by the patched reference loads into it as before.
diff --git a/TPMCmd/tpm/src/subsystem/Object.c b/TPMCmd/tpm/src/subsystem/Object.c
--- a/TPMCmd/tpm/src/subsystem/Object.c
+++ b/TPMCmd/tpm/src/subsystem/Object.c
@@ -195,7 +195,12 @@
             if(handle)
                 *handle = i + TRANSIENT_FIRST;
             // Initialize the object attributes
-            MemorySet(&object->attributes, 0, sizeof(OBJECT_ATTRIBUTES));
+            // tpm-rs differential testing -- harness (determinism): the whole
+            // slot is cleared (as ibmswtpm2/libtpms and v185 do), not only its
+            // attributes, so that TPM2_ContextSave() does not copy octets a
+            // former occupant of the slot left behind (e.g. the tail of a
+            // larger hash state behind the SHA-1 state of a sequence object).
+            MemorySet(object, 0, sizeof(*object));
             object->hierarchy = TPM_RH_NULL;
             return object;
         }
